				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/afx-neuromancer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2739221); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2739221&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200933" alt="AFX Neuromancer" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2739221)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/afx-neuromancer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2739221); return false;" >AFX Neuromancer</a></span> <span class="odate time_1547093482 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Jan 2019 04:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4114317">
									

<p>I dunno&#8230; I thought your clinical tone was pretty good throughout.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Top&nbsp;10&nbsp;Ideas&nbsp;for&nbsp;Countdown&nbsp;Shows</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Whose&nbsp;culture&nbsp;is&nbsp;this&nbsp;and&nbsp;does&nbsp;anybody&nbsp;know?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4682 is to remain wrapped in a plastic tarp to prevent individuals from looking looking at or being reflected in it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Don't explain the reasoning behind the containment procedures, at least not in the containment procedure section (you <em>do</em> explain this later in the description). So get rid of the &quot;to prevent individuals from looking at it (you also have a repeat &quot;looking&quot; in there)). I'd recommend changing this to something like &quot;SCP-4682 is to remain wrapped within any opaque material. The viewing of ones own image in SCP-4682 is prohibited.&quot; Something like that, anyway. It ain't great, needs work, but you get the idea.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>at all costs</p>
</blockquote>
<p>get rid of this. This is the only real example I could spot where your clinical tone lapses.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>are restricted from appearing in any visual form of monitoring</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like this is worded oddly. Maybe &quot;restricted from appearing in any visual media&quot;? I'm not sure if media is the right word here, but I think it might be.</p>
<p>One small typo:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>they will perceive a minor physical deformities or imperfections in their appearance</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;a minor&quot; is singular, so deformity or imperfection should be singular, too (just get rid of the &quot;a&quot; and you can keep them plural).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>these imperfections are present in video and artistic or photographic depictions</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;artistic&quot; depiction has got me hung up. I'm trying to figure out how that would work. Assuming the artist observed the affected person with their naked eye, wouldn't their depiction then be unimpacted by this?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Rotting or yellowing teeth, and occasionally, cavities;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why just occassionally cavities? That seems a far less severe imperfection than rotting teeth.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This effect is cumulative: whenever an affected person sees themselves in a reflective surface (such as another mirror, a blank computer screen, or a polished surface) they will perceive another imperfection each additional time they view their own reflection.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you need to expand on this part a little, just for clarification to the reader, because it seems like not only are the effects cumulative, but they're also communicable to other reflective surfaces.</p>
<p>That's it for the line-by-line, and all of it's pretty minor hiccups that can be easily fixed. Certainly not enough to discourage me from upvoting.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I'm not usually a fan of the closing diary page or email or voice message, and especially not the suicide note, but you surprised me pleasantly&#8230; I thought the note was pretty well-written and believable. It was sad without being histrionic. Nice work, 'cause that's not easy to pull off.</p>
<p>Hope your night gets better, and looking forward to more from you!</p>

							</div>

								
					
